A||||8||CUTTER||Hard starting question for 85GT||||1||
Z||000000||CUTTER||06-24-2000||07:06 PM||||My friend wants to buy this white 85GT and we talked to the guy last night and he said that it starts hard, he said you have to turn the key on about 3 0r 4 times to get the pressure up then it starts. He said its something like the fuel pump doesnt run long enough when you turn the key and it shuts off early. What does this sound like? and around how much to fix? He said when its started it runs fine. What could it be?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000001||Terrybogin||06-24-2000||07:10 PM||||The car has two pump relays. One runs the pump for a few seconds when you first turn on the key. The other runs the pump when oil pressure is built up enough to turn off the oil light. If the first relay is bad, then you will have to crank the engine over long enough to build up oil pressure before it will start.||||reg||1
Z||000002||CUTTER||06-25-2000||05:57 AM||||where is that relay located?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||06-26-2000||07:26 AM||||Terrybogin,<P>My 89 Bonneville does the same.  I replaced the plugs (they were well worn to almost .080") and it got better but it still doesn't start on the first crank.  Do you know if Pontiac has the same dual relay setup on the Bonneville?<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||daved||06-26-2000||11:02 PM||||The fuel pump relay is in the engine compartment, on the firewall behind the driver.  On my V6, its almost hidden by the the air filter stuff and is the relay on the right. Dont know what the other relay does. See Haynes, pg.132, pic 3.6 .<BR>The schematic wiring diagram is on page 276, the lower diagram for the 4 cyl, and pg. 278 lower diagram for the 6cyl. <BR> Note that there is only ONE relay for the fuel pump, the oil pressure switch being in parallel (across) the relay contacts takes over once the engine builds up enough pressure.  The relay is energised by the ECM.<BR>Hope this helps.  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||06-27-2000||07:25 AM||||I looked up the wiring diagram on my 89 Bonneville and it is basically the same as the Fiero.  Relay is used to energize the fuel pump during cranking before oil pressure builds up.  If it does not work, the fuel pump will run after the oil pressure reaches 4 psi.||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||06-27-2000||11:07 AM||||Another possibility to be considered. Find out the last time the pickup coil and module were changed out on the distributer. A bad PU coil can do the same thing to starting. I have never heard of a fuel pump shutting off before or not enough. If it was the pump you would show low preasure and it would very soon affect the every day driving. To find out for sure slap a preasure gage and see. <P>Oh and I can't stress enough to always use AC Delco parts for the distributer. With the PU coil and Module you are essentially rebuilding your distributer. They aren't cheap but they are much more reliable in the long term over aftermarket stuff. <P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||06-27-2000||11:08 AM||||Another possibility to be considered. Find out the last time the pickup coil and module were changed out on the distributer. A bad PU coil can do the same thing to starting. I have never heard of a fuel pump shutting off before or not enough. If it was the pump you would show low preasure and it would very soon affect the every day driving. To find out for sure slap a preasure gauge and see. <P>Oh and I can't stress enough to always use AC Delco parts for the distributer. With the PU coil and Module you are essentially rebuilding your distributer. They aren't cheap but they are much more reliable in the long term over aftermarket stuff. <P>Good luck!||||reg||1
